WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Authorization architecture defines where decisions are made and where they are enforced.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:09.080
A policy decision point evaluates policy and context.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.820
A policy enforcement point applies the result by allowing or denying access.

4
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:26.320
In a Python application, these roles might be implemented through local functions, shared libraries, framework hooks, a policy engine, a database rule layer, or a centralized authorization service.

5
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.920
Local checks can be simple, fast, and close to application code.

6
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.260
Centralized policy services can improve consistency across multiple services.

7
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.080
Policy engines can help express rules outside individual handlers.

8
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:47.220
Each pattern has tradeoffs in performance, clarity, availability, and operational complexity.

9
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.920
The right architecture should make safe behavior consistent and hard to bypass accidentally, not merely fashionable.

10
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.400
Data filtering is a common authorization pattern for list endpoints and search results.

11
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.380
A caller should only receive records they are allowed to see.

12
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.760
Filtering needs to happen in the backend query or service logic, not just in the client.

13
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:12.300
Overbroad query results can expose sensitive data even if individual record pages have checks.

14
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:20.080
The list view, export path, API endpoint, and report generator should all follow the same access expectations.

15
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:25.000
Caching authorization decisions can improve performance, but stale permissions create risk.

16
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.780
Roles, permissions, ownership, tenant membership, and policy versions can change.

17
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:38.240
If cached decisions are used, the design should account for lifetime, invalidation, policy versioning, and fail-closed behavior.

18
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:45.220
If the policy system cannot evaluate access safely, the safer default is generally to deny rather than allow.

19
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:51.000
Error handling should support operators without revealing sensitive policy internals to unauthorized callers.
